      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The goal is to find spherical coordinates for a point in rectangular coordinates given by $(x,y,z) = (0,-3 \\sqrt{3},3).$ To do so, we need to convert the rectangular coordinates into well behaved spherical coordinates as indicated by the problem. We wish to solve the problem for $\\rho > 0,$ $0 \\le \\theta < 2 \\pi,$ and $0 \\le \\phi \\le \\pi.$ This means we should complete the following conversion:\n$$ x = \\rho \\sin \\theta \\cos \\phi \\tag{1}\\\\\ny = \\rho \\sin \\theta \\sin \\phi \\tag{2}\\\\\nz = \\rho \\cos \\theta \\tag{3}$$\nFurthermore, $\\rho$ is defined in terms of the $x$ and $y$ components by \n$$ \\rho^2 = x^2 + y^2,$$\nso\n$$ \\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}. \\tag{4}$$\nFinally, \n$\\theta$ is defined by (2) with $z=0$ given by \n$$ x = \\rho \\sin \\theta \\cos \\phi \\tag{5}\n$$\nso it can equal any angle when $y = 0$ in the horizontal plane above z=0. If we want $\\theta$ to be less than $2 \\pi$ we can restrict (knowing that $\\theta = 0$ is on the negative z axis and $2 \\pi$ is on the top positive z axis; hence, if no restriction is made then $\\theta = 2 \\pi$ is also on the positive z axis). Lastly, we need to solve for $\\phi$ given the $z$ value\n$$ z = \\rho \\cos \\theta \\tag {6}\n$$\nso\n$$ \\phi = \\arcsin (z/\\rho). \\tag{$\\star$}$$ This solution can be substituted back into other expressions since $\\phi$ is not dependent on $x$ or $y$.\nWe begin by solving for $\\rho$: \n$\\begin{align} z & = \\rho \\cos \\theta \\tag{$\\star$}\\\\\n\\frac{z}{\\rho} & = \\cos \\theta \\tag{1} \\\\\n\\frac{3}{\\rho} & = \\cos \\theta \\tag{1}\\\\\n\\text{so} \\quad \\displaystyle \\rho & = \\frac{3}{\\cos \\theta}. \\tag{7}\n\\end{align}$\nNext we have\n\\begin{align} x^2 + y^2 & = \\rho^2 = \\frac{9}{\\cos^2 \\theta}\\\\\n3 \\cos^2 \\theta - x^2 & = y^2.\n\\end{align}\nWe accept all those values of $\\theta$ for which $x^2$ is smaller than $y^2$ or for which\n$$ 3\\cos^2 \\theta - x^2 < y^2 \\tag{8}$$\nusing the two inequalities,\n$$ 3 \\cos^2 \\theta - 3 < 0, $$\nAND $y^2 \\ge 0,$\nso\n$$ 0 < 3 \\cos^2 \\theta - x^2 < y^2 \\tag{$\\wedge$}$$\nis satisfied for all $x,y$ with\n$$ 0 < \\rho < 3 \\label{ineq1},  \\tag{10}$$\nwhere $\\rho$ is positive and $\\theta$ is NOT defined for $\\rho = 0.$ Second, we obtain a constraint for $\\phi$:\n$$ z = \\rho \\cos \\phi = \\frac{3 \\cos \\theta}{\\cos \\theta} = 3\\cos \\theta, $$\nwhich is positive by $\\eqref{ineq}$. Lastly, \n  \\begin{align}\n0 \\le \\theta &< 2 \\pi \\\\\n\\text{and} \\quad 3 \\cos^2 \\theta - x^2 < y^2 \\quad \\textrm{and} \n\\end{align}\nare all we need to get $\\phi$.\n\\begin{align}\n\\tan \\theta & = \\frac{x}{y} \\\\\n\\tan \\theta & = \\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\\n\\theta & = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}. & \\text{since }0<\\theta<2 \\pi \\\\\n\\rho & = \\frac{3}{\\cos \\theta} =1.\\nonumber \\\\\n\\phi & = \\arcsin (y/\\rho) =  \\arcsin (1/\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\tag{$\\star$} \\nonumber\n\\end{align}\nshowing that $\\theta = 2 \\pi -\\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{4\\pi}{3}$ \nyields the same set of coordinates. So, our answer is:\n$$\\boxed{(1, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{6})} = \\boxed{(1, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{6})} = (x,y,z) = (0,-3 \\sqrt{3},3).$$",
